<div class="summary">
    <p>
        A collaboration with two Virginia Tech faculty, one from VR and human-computer interaction and one from
        quantum computing, on whether an interactive sandbox teaches quantum fundamentals better than a lecture.
        I built the VR side. The project ended early and that portion was cut; what shipped was a rough desktop
        version from the other half of the team.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>The idea</h2>
<p>
    Quantum computing is hard to teach because its core ideas do not map onto everyday physical intuition. A
    qubit in superposition is not simply a one or a zero, and entanglement links two qubits’ states in a way
    nothing in a classical computer does. Most instruction still leans on the math first or, at best, on
    two-dimensional circuit simulators. The bet here was that composing a circuit yourself, dragging qubits
    through Hadamard and CNOT gates and watching the state change, builds that intuition faster than watching
    someone explain it.
</p>

<h2>What it was meant to do</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Teach the counter-intuitive parts first: superposition, the plus and minus states, and entanglement.</li>
    <li>Cover real gate mechanics rather than metaphors, including Hadamard and conditional gates like CNOT.</li>
    <li>Be a sandbox, not a lesson. Students build their own circuits, and the interactivity is the pedagogy.</li>
    <li>Run on two platforms, a VR build and a parallel desktop version, so the approaches could be compared.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What actually happened</h2>
<p>
    The VR portion was never finished. My side was cut when the project wound down, and the surviving artefact
    is a basic desktop sandbox built by the other team, functional at a rough level. I keep it on this page
    anyway. The research question was real, it is the only quantum and VR work I have done, and paired with the
    <a href="/experience/#smart">Vision Pro work at SMART</a> it is what turns one headset project into an
    actual interest in spatial interfaces.
</p>

